  "worked_solution": "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{\u2202f}{\u2202x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{\u2202f}{\u2202y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhere $\u27e8\\frac{\u2202f}{\u2202x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\\frac{\u2202f}{\u2202y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),-1\u27e9$ represents a normal vector to the tangent plane.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\u2202f}{\u2202x} & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202x}(3xy-\\operatorname{ln}\u2061y) \\\\  & =3y \\\\ \\frac{\u2202f}{\u2202y} & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}(3xy-\\operatorname{ln}\u2061y) \\\\  & =3x-\\frac{1}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the normal vector to the tangent plane is \n\n$$\n\u27e83y,\\,3x-\\frac{1}{y},\\,-1\u27e9\n$$\n\nOur tangent plane should be perpendicular to $\u27e83,5,-1\u27e9.$ So, the normal vector of that plane must be parallel to that vector. In other words, for some constant $c$, we must have\n\n$$\nc\\cdot \u27e83y,\\,3x-\\frac{1}{y},\\,-1\u27e9=\u27e83,5,-1\u27e9.\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding coordinates, we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3cy=3 \\\\ 3cx-\\frac{c}{y}=5 \\\\ -c=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get $c=1.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n3(1)y=3\\,\u27f9\\,y=1.\n$$\n\nAnd substituting $c=1$ and $y=1$ into the second equation, we have \n\n$$\n3(1)x-\\frac{1}{1}=5\\,\u27f9\\,x=2.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=2$ and $y=1$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =3xy-\\operatorname{ln}\u2061y \\\\  & =3(2)(1)-\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(1) \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent plane is perpendicular to the vector  $v$ at $(2,1,6).$",
